From ca549e30b72b26c0200fee2b0ecd0977556132d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:33:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] i686: scope never-read-store DSE per function; widen
 folded-operand read ranges

The i686 eliminate_never_read_stores ran over the whole module buffer:
a read of 24(%esp) in ANY function kept a same-offset dead store alive
in EVERY function, and the inter-function labels invalidated the linear
ESP walk (esp_walk_ok=false) for the entire TU. The x86-64 twin was
already per-function. Split at .cfi_endproc/.size markers, analysis and
rewrite confined to [start,end).

Scoping exposed a latent undersizing bug: folded-operand frame reads
(infos[i].ebp_offset from lines like 'fldl 16(%esp)') were recorded as
4-byte reads, but x87 loads read 8 (fldl) or 10 (fldt) bytes. With the
pass now firing inside regparm capture sequences it deleted the live
high-dword store of a double parameter (d_i(double,int) miscompile,
regparm3_abi_conformance bit 5 at -O1+). Folded reads are now recorded
as 16 bytes, covering every x86 memory read that names the offset as
its lowest address.

Two unit tests pin both behaviors. Realmode boot corpus: 34491 -> 34389
bytes (gcc 12999). 837/837 unit, 50/50 correctness, 9/9 check scripts,
regparm conformance all levels.
