From 4b72f16f4381d8c39d63094709741be1b109a08f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 13:18:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] i686: unused callee-save elimination for multi-epilogue
 and frameless functions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The old pass demanded exactly one subl alloc and one addl dealloc, so a
function with an early-return epilogue (get_cpuflags, go_to_protected_mode)
or no frame at all (bcmp after tail-call conversion) kept every dead
push/pop pair. The setup corpus had 20 such pairs across 12 functions.

Rewritten with two proven shapes:
 * FRAMED: one subl alloc, 1..n addl deallocs of the SAME value, each
   epilogue restoring the register exactly once, pops qualified as
   epilogue pops (only pops/directives between them and a dealloc).
   Removal grows alloc AND every dealloc by 4/pair, so body ESP and all
   N(%esp) slots are untouched.
 * FRAMELESS: no ESP arithmetic AND no (%esp) reference anywhere — with
   no slots, shifting body ESP by the removed pairs is unobservable.
   bcmp collapses from 10 instructions to 'jmp memcmp'.

Anything else (mismatched dealloc values, mixed shapes) is left alone.
The register must be unreferenced outside its own push/pop lines; the
duplicate-push guard stays.

test_tail_call_direct_i686 updated: its %ebx pair is dead by liveness,
so the full collapse to 'jmp target' is now the EXPECTED result; a new
sibling test pins the restore-before-jmp order with a LIVE callee-save.
4 more unit tests pin frameless removal, the esp-slot suppression, and
the multi-epilogue +4 rewrite.

Realmode boot corpus: 33194 -> 33138 bytes. 848/848 unit, 50/50
correctness, 9/9 checks, regparm conformance all levels, fnptr runtime
matrix, nbody -O0 exact.
